WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.005
Picture a busy café.

2
00:00:04.005 --> 00:00:07.919
In the morning, the owner opens the café's app on her phone.

3
00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:12.248
Her balance, the money her card sales have brought in, says 49.00.

4
00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:15.667
But an hour ago, she refunded a 40.00 order.

5
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:17.644
So the balance should be 9.00.

6
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.276
Same app, same payments, two answers.

7
00:00:20.276 --> 00:00:21.433
Which one is true?

8
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:23.552
And how do you fix the other one?

9
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:27.090
The tempting fix is to type 9.00 over the 49.00.

10
00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:31.229
It's the number everyone sees, so it feels like the real one.

11
00:00:31.229 --> 00:00:32.451
Hold that thought.

12
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:36.047
To see which is true, picture the café without the app.

13
00:00:36.047 --> 00:00:38.336
It keeps a paper notebook by the till.

14
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.259
Every sale goes on the next empty line: which card paid, and how much.

15
00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:45.056
Nothing is ever rubbed out.

16
00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:47.121
Two helpers grow around it.

17
00:00:47.121 --> 00:00:50.829
At the back, an index: each card, and the lines it's on.

18
00:00:51.067 --> 00:00:57.580
And by the till, a sticky note with the balance, so nobody adds up the notebook every time someone asks.

19
00:00:57.833 --> 00:00:59.216
Now, this is a café.

20
00:00:59.216 --> 00:01:02.604
Sooner or later, coffee lands on the sticky note.

21
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:04.500
Does the owner panic?

22
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:04.719
No.

23
00:01:04.719 --> 00:01:08.149
She adds up the notebook and writes a new note.

24
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:13.017
She'd never do it the other way round, and change the notebook to match the note.

25
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.987
The app works the same way.

26
00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:20.235
The program behind the card reader adds each payment as a line at the end of a file.

27
00:01:20.235 --> 00:01:23.859
That file is the notebook, and the app keeps an index too.

28
00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:25.926
And the balance on her phone?

29
00:01:25.926 --> 00:01:28.523
The app doesn't add up the file each time.

30
00:01:28.523 --> 00:01:31.059
It reads a saved answer, its sticky note.

31
00:01:31.059 --> 00:01:33.717
A saved answer like that is called a cache.

32
00:01:33.967 --> 00:01:35.603
Why keep copies at all?

33
00:01:35.603 --> 00:01:38.948
Adding up millions of lines every time is slow.

34
00:01:38.948 --> 00:01:41.368
Each copy makes one question fast.

35
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:43.698
So which of these is the truth?

36
00:01:43.698 --> 00:01:44.630
Here's a test.

37
00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:47.827
If a box were thrown away, could we get it back?

38
00:01:47.827 --> 00:01:48.892
Pause and guess.

39
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:53.329
The index: yes.

40
00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:55.243
Read the notebook again.

41
00:01:55.243 --> 00:01:56.918
The sticky note: yes.

42
00:01:56.918 --> 00:01:58.752
We saw the owner do it.

43
00:01:58.967 --> 00:02:00.036
The notebook: no.

44
00:02:00.036 --> 00:02:03.433
The note knows one number, not which payments made it.

45
00:02:03.433 --> 00:02:06.389
Lose the notebook, and those payments are gone.

46
00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:10.888
The notebook is the system of record, also called the source of truth.

47
00:02:10.888 --> 00:02:13.481
Each fact is written there first, and once.

48
00:02:13.481 --> 00:02:16.436
When anything disagrees with it, the record wins.

49
00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:23.564
The index and the cache are derived data: worked out from the record, so they can be thrown away and worked out again.

50
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.700
So how did the app get 49.00?

51
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.059
Here the café picture stops working.

52
00:02:28.059 --> 00:02:30.353
The owner updated her note by hand.

53
00:02:30.353 --> 00:02:35.005
The program updates its copies on its own, thousands of times a second.

54
00:02:35.233 --> 00:02:36.443
Watch the refund.

55
00:02:36.443 --> 00:02:39.503
It goes into the file first, as a new line.

56
00:02:39.503 --> 00:02:41.993
Then the program updates each copy.

57
00:02:41.993 --> 00:02:43.559
The index adds line 3.

58
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.587
The cache gets a short message: forget your saved balance.

59
00:02:47.587 --> 00:02:50.003
But that message got lost on the way.

60
00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:52.353
So the cache kept handing out 49.00.

61
00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:55.876
A copy that missed the newest record is called stale.

62
00:02:55.876 --> 00:02:57.999
And nothing on the screen says so.

63
00:02:57.999 --> 00:03:01.184
A stale 49.00 looks just as sure of itself as 9.00.

64
00:03:01.433 --> 00:03:02.861
Now, the tempting fix.

65
00:03:02.861 --> 00:03:04.418
Type 9.00 over the note.

66
00:03:04.418 --> 00:03:07.597
To be fair, the phone now shows the right number.

67
00:03:07.833 --> 00:03:09.512
But where did you get 9.00?

68
00:03:09.512 --> 00:03:11.129
You added up the notebook.

69
00:03:11.129 --> 00:03:13.616
So the notebook was the judge all along.

70
00:03:13.616 --> 00:03:18.591
Typing it in is a rebuild done by hand, for the one number you happened to spot.

71
00:03:18.833 --> 00:03:22.645
And follow the idea underneath, that the screen is the truth.

72
00:03:22.645 --> 00:03:26.083
Then the notebook must be wrong, so rub out the refund.

73
00:03:26.083 --> 00:03:30.395
Now everything agrees on 49.00, and a customer's refund has vanished.

74
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:31.146
Well...

75
00:03:31.146 --> 00:03:33.408
that's not the fix we wanted.

76
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.759
Let's undo that, and do what the owner did after the coffee.

77
00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:39.174
Throw the copies away.

78
00:03:39.174 --> 00:03:42.325
Nothing is lost, because the file hasn't changed.

79
00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:48.442
Read the file from the first line, in order, and work each copy out again.

80
00:03:48.442 --> 00:03:48.919
40.00.

81
00:03:48.919 --> 00:03:49.395
49.00.

82
00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:51.063
And the refund: 9.00.

83
00:03:52.033 --> 00:03:53.965
That's called replaying the record.

84
00:03:53.965 --> 00:03:58.105
The rebuild didn't need to know what went wrong, and nobody typed a number.

85
00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.414
Two habits keep this safe.

86
00:04:00.414 --> 00:04:03.380
Write the record first and the copies second.

87
00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:08.917
If the power cuts out in between, a copy is only behind, and a replay catches it up.

88
00:04:09.133 --> 00:04:10.803
And never rub out a line.

89
00:04:10.803 --> 00:04:14.075
A rebuild is only as good as the record it reads.

90
00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:17.345
One cost: a rebuild reads the whole record.

91
00:04:17.345 --> 00:04:19.077
Three lines take no time.

92
00:04:19.077 --> 00:04:21.363
Years of payments can take hours.

93
00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:22.847
So, 49.00 or 9.00?

94
00:04:22.847 --> 00:04:27.505
Every copy is worked out from the notebook, and the notebook says 9.00.

95
00:04:27.505 --> 00:04:31.244
It wins, even when the sticky note has nicer handwriting.

96
00:04:31.467 --> 00:04:34.551
A copy that disagrees with the record is wrong.

97
00:04:34.551 --> 00:04:36.388
Rebuild it, don't retype it.

98
00:04:36.388 --> 00:04:41.769
And of any data you meet, ask: is this where the fact is decided, or a copy of it?

99
00:04:42.333 --> 00:04:43.427
One quick check.

100
00:04:43.427 --> 00:04:48.008
The index says a card is on line 5, but the notebook has no line 5.

101
00:04:48.008 --> 00:04:50.674
Which one is wrong, and what do you do?

102
00:04:50.674 --> 00:04:51.768
Pause and think.

103
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.311
The index is wrong.

104
00:04:56.311 --> 00:04:59.003
Throw it away, and replay the notebook.
